My name is Chris Latimer.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.900
I'm a product manager
here at Google.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.174
I focus on is an API management.

00:00:19.174 --> 00:00:20.840
And I'm joined by my
esteemed colleague.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:21.506
SCOTT GANYO: Hi.

00:00:21.506 --> 00:00:22.880
My name is Scott Ganyo.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:23.940
I'm a software engineer.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.440
And I've been working
on the adapter

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.990
that you're about
to see for Mixer.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:28.490
CHRIS LATIMER: And
today we're going

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.750
to be talking about the role
of API management and the Istio

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:32.780
service mesh.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.710
So let's start off by talking
about what a service mesh is

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:39.200
and why we need one.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.290
So if you're like
most companies,

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.040
you probably have a
lot of people thinking

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.360
that microservices are awesome.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.660
You're probably looking at
ways to adopt microservices

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.360
and build a microservices-based
architecture.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.150
But why exactly do we
need microservices?

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.490
If you think about the
alternative, what we typically

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.710
call a monolith, there's
a lot of good things

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:58.524
about a monolith.

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:00.565
For one thing, it's usually
in one big code base.

00:01:00.565 --> 00:01:04.019
So if you're going to work on
a monolith, it's really easy.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:05.951
We just have to clone one repo.

00:01:05.951 --> 00:01:07.700
We're usually working
on a single project.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.220
We're usually building just
one thing, running one thing.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.040
It's really easy to get
started with a monolith.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.104
Usually, where monoliths
break down is that they grow

00:01:16.104 --> 00:01:18.020
and they keep growing
and growing and growing,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.480
and not just from a code
base standpoint, but also

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.104
the number of people that
are working on monoliths.

00:01:23.104 --> 00:01:25.520
And so when that happens, we
start to run into challenges.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.370
Usually, we start to have
to coordinate our releases.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.929
And when we look at
really big companies that

00:01:30.929 --> 00:01:32.720
are building these
monoliths, these mission

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.340
critical monoliths,
it's not uncommon

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.860
that they can only release
two or three times a year.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.725
And so when we're trying
to get increased agility

00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:42.350
from our organization,
it's really hard

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.725
to do with a
monolith, especially

00:01:43.725 --> 00:01:44.990
as they start to grow.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.300
And it's around that point
that most organizations

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.510
start to look at microservices.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.100
So microservices have
a number of advantages.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.834
They address a lot
of the concerns

00:01:54.834 --> 00:01:56.000
that we have with monoliths.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.460
So instead of having
one big code base,

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.540
we can decompose a monolith
into a number of microservices.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.300
These microservices usually have
some kind of bounded context.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.340
We have a dedicated team
that works on these.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.810
They're independently
deployable,

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.699
independently scalable.

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:13.070
And when we start to
look at orchestrating

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:16.856
all of these microservices to
work together to form a system,

00:02:16.856 --> 00:02:18.230
we usually think
of this or refer

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.050
to this as a service mesh.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.840
Now, this isn't to
say that microservices

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.560
are a perfect solution.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.140
If you look at what
happens as microservices

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.620
grow and your service
mesh starts to grow,

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.260
you'll run into a
number of challenges.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.710
So when we try to look at
things like dependencies

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.900
across services,
this is fairly easy

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.880
when you have a small
set of microservices.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.190
As they start to
increase, figuring out

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.477
what depends on
something else is tricky.

00:02:46.477 --> 00:02:48.560
One of the things that we
like about microservices

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.690
is that they are
independently deployable.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:52.910
So if you have a change
to a microservice,

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.590
you can deploy that
without necessarily

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.900
waiting for everything
else to be deployed.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.947
But that's not always the case.

00:02:58.947 --> 00:03:00.530
You may have situations
where you have

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:01.850
dependencies on these services.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.909
So if you have a
change that's breaking,

00:03:03.909 --> 00:03:05.450
you want to understand
who's breaking

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.020
and you want to have a way to
manage those dependencies so

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.910
that you can
minimize the impact.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:13.879
The other thing is,
with the monolith,

00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:15.420
if we all of a sudden
have a problem,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.795
maybe an exception
happens, we've

00:03:16.795 --> 00:03:19.310
got a stack trace that tells
us the entire application.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.770
Where did this problem
originate from?

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.260
When you're looking across
the mesh of microservices,

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.240
that's a trickier
problem to solve,

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.910
to be able to pinpoint exactly
where the problem is occurring.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.490
And so as you start
to look at those,

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.077
those are some hard
problems to solve.

00:03:33.077 --> 00:03:35.160
And you might be thinking
to yourself, well, gosh,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.100
are microservices that great?

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.890
And the good news is just
about every technology

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.629
that we've ever
used has some type

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:43.670
of challenge associated
with it and microservices

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:44.692
are no different.

00:03:44.692 --> 00:03:46.400
And when that happens,
what we tend to do

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.150
is we look to build out tools
and frameworks and platforms

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.280
that are going to solve
hard problems for us

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:55.160
so that we can focus on the
business value and the business

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:56.840
logic that we want
to build and we want

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.150
to run in our microservices.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:02.690
And that's really what Istio
is trying to do for us.

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:08.150
And there are-- so let me talk a
little bit what Istio is first.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.970
So it's an open source platform
for solving these problems

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:12.780
that we've just described.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:16.686
It's a collaboration between
Google, IBM, Lyft, Cisco--

00:04:16.686 --> 00:04:18.560
many other companies
are all working together

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.740
to build out this platform.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.420
And the platform really
has three main areas

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.140
that it looks to address.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.780
So the first is
around observability.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.577
So if you think
about microservices,

00:04:28.577 --> 00:04:30.410
we're building these
in different languages.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.050
We might be building
microservices in Go, in Java,

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:37.460
C++, Python, whatever
language you may prefer.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.470
But all those microservices--

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.109
we want to be able to look
at a common set of data.

00:04:42.109 --> 00:04:44.150
We want to be able to look
across those services,

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.280
understand how are
they performing.

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:48.980
Are we seeing an uptick in
errors across these services?

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:51.389
How much traffic is flowing
through these microservices?

00:04:51.389 --> 00:04:53.180
And we don't necessarily
want to build that

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.956
into each and every
microservice individually.

00:04:55.956 --> 00:04:57.830
What we would rather do
is delegate that down

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.750
to a platform.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.210
In this case, Istio provides
that capability for you

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:05.387
to solve that problem.

00:05:05.387 --> 00:05:06.970
The other challenge
is around agility.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.010
So again, I mentioned
if all of a sudden

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.156
we're deploying a new
version of a microservice,

00:05:11.156 --> 00:05:13.030
we don't necessarily
want to break everything

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.570
that depends on that.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.240
And so in order to
accomplish that,

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.029
we might want to do things
like roll out a canary version.

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:23.320
So we want to see if we
actually break something, maybe

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.480
send a small percentage
of traffic to it.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:28.190
We want to handle routing
to the appropriate service,

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.040
to the appropriate
version of the service.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.590
Again, that becomes
very easy with Istio.

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:34.654
And then, of course,
just like anything else

00:05:34.654 --> 00:05:36.070
we different
technical standpoint,

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.600
security is going to
be a paramount concern.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.550
And we want to be able to
apply security very easily.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:45.450
So the way that we
accomplish this in Istio

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.650
is with three main
control plane components.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:48.896
We've got Pilot.

00:05:48.896 --> 00:05:51.270
Pilot's going to be responsible
for all the configuration

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:51.940
of Istio.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.390
So when you're ready to
deploy a microservice

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:58.200
and apply policies to it, you're
going to interact with Pilot.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.747
Now, those policies are
going to be enforced

00:06:00.747 --> 00:06:01.830
by something called Mixer.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.540
So normally, we
want to apply things

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.420
like traffic management
or a rate limiting.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.190
We want to put in
place security checks.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.990
The way that those
security checks are defined

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.790
as in policies and the
way they're enforced

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:13.830
is with a component
called Mixer,

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.770
and we'll talk a little bit
more about Mixer coming up.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.240
And then, finally,
we have Citadel.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.060
So Citadel is what's going
to handle all of the network

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:21.960
security for us.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.060
It's going to provide
service-to-service

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.200
authentication authorization
using mutual TLS.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:33.439
So what exactly can we do with
the security aspects of Istio?

00:06:33.439 --> 00:06:34.980
Well, the first
thing is we are going

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:38.400
to be able to use
mutual TLS to identify

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.810
the service and the
service consumer.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:42.450
So because it's
mutual TLS, we're

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.730
going to have assurance
that we know exactly which

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:46.890
services is making a
call in and we're also

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:48.990
going to be able to apply
authorization rules.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:50.460
So we're going to
be able to say,

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.000
is this service allowed to call
another service in our mesh?

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.260
And we could even go one
degree finer as well.

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.610
We can actually have
fine-grained rules

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.130
around that service, the
service communication.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.900
So maybe I have
an invoice service

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.180
and that invoice service
has two types of consumers.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.880
Maybe one of those
consumers is allowed

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.080
to have a read-only access.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.360
Maybe one's allowed
to have full access.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:17.970
And with Istio, we're able
to provide the capabilities

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:22.100
for applying those rules.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.970
Now, those rules are
actually enforced by Mixer.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:28.990
So with Mixer what we
can do is we can say,

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:30.580
we've got Citadel,
which has provided

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.290
a secure channel
of communication

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:36.056
across all of these different
service interactions,

00:07:36.056 --> 00:07:37.930
but we may also have
policy-based enforcement

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:39.820
that we want to apply as well.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:42.430
Mixer is the extensible
platform that allows

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.380
us to enforce these policies.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.870
So Mixer has a number
of integrations

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.360
that are pre-built. So
we've got integrations

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:53.120
with Apigee, with Stackdriver,
with Zipkin, with many others.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.430
And so it is very easy for
us to take a microservice

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.360
and quickly apply
these policies,

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:01.180
quickly apply
transport-level security

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.790
across all the communication.

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:06.220
And all of that is
configured using Pilot.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:09.040
So Pilot is going to be the
mechanism that we interact

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:11.800
with that's going to take
all of those components

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.590
and allow us to push a
configuration across it

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.770
across everything.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:18.920
And even though these
may sound like there's

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.590
a lot of things going on,
we've made it extremely easy

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.050
for you to use with Istio.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:27.470
All you need to do is write
a few snippets of YAML

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:30.470
to describe your service, to
describe the policies that you

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.659
want to enforce on
the services, push

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:36.500
those in and all of a sudden,
Istio takes care of the rest.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:39.980
So you've now got a
feeling for what Istio is

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:41.640
and all the components.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.080
Let's take a look now at how
it actually works in practice.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:45.890
SCOTT GANYO: OK.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.100
Wow.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:51.800
As you can see, Istio
does a lot for you, right?

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:54.380
I'm not going to have time
to go through all of that

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.510
because we're going to want to
get to API management in a bit.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.680
But I do want to show you
a little bit about what

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:01.960
Istio does.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:03.900
And this this is
the Bookinfo app.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.600
This is the architecture for it.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.430
It's a simple app
that comes with Istio.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:12.180
As you can see, it just has
a page that you can hit.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:16.220
It has a login thing and a
bunch of different versions

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:19.010
of a review service.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.710
And one of the features,
as you talked about

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:23.690
with the microservices,
is all of these

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:26.670
are running in
different environments,

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.670
different languages.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:32.330
So we have Python, Java, node,
Ruby all working together

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:33.790
for this one app.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:35.150
So let's get in.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.300
Let me show you.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:42.070
So what I did here is I just
installed Istio on Kubernetes--

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:48.750
Bookinfo and a gateway so
that you can access it.

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:53.010
And I'm just going to show
you what that page looks like.

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:55.460
So this is the details
page for one of the books,

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:57.080
"The Comedy Of Errors."

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:00.740
And as I refresh,
what you'll see

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:06.470
is different versions
of the review service.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:09.759
Now normally, you might
go in and, as you said,

00:10:09.759 --> 00:10:11.300
you might have a
canary or something.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:18.320
Like, I want to take version
1 and then apply version 2 to,

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:19.310
like, 20% of traffic.

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:22.280
Or maybe just a tester
can access that.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:23.940
And so I can do all that.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:27.460
But what I want to
show you is some

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:30.400
of the cool stuff that
happens after the fact

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.020
in the telemetry.

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:35.350
And we have all
these plug-ins that

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:38.740
are available for Jaeger,
ServiceGraph, Grafana,

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:39.555
Prometheus.

00:10:39.555 --> 00:10:43.700
And I just wanted to show you,
for example, the ServiceGraph.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:46.240
So when I was hitting
refresh on that page earlier,

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.360
this was actually
being generated.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:53.290
This actually shows
you how your request

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.490
is traveling through
the system and which

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:57.490
versions you're hitting.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:02.320
You can see review version
3, ratings, version 1,

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.390
things like that.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:03.520
CHRIS LATIMER: Yeah.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:04.840
This is like what we
were talking earlier

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:06.340
about the problems
with microservices

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.423
and really understanding
what's dependent on what.

00:11:08.423 --> 00:11:11.560
This just makes it crystal
clear exactly what's being used.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:13.870
SCOTT GANYO: So you can see
exactly what's going on.

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:18.100
In addition-- now let me
show you the Jaeger UI.

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:19.710
You can actually see traces.

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:23.830
So if I take and pick
one of my requests,

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:26.440
I can actually see the
breakdown of how long it

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:30.220
took at each service and
which service forwarded on

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:31.050
to the next.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:31.180
CHRIS LATIMER: Yeah.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.860
I mean, you can
imagine if you're

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:34.720
managing a set of
these microservices

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:37.010
and you get a call from the
help desk that says, hey,

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.490
this application is
starting to run really slow.

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:42.875
Rather than have to go in
and try to drill into logs

00:11:42.875 --> 00:11:44.500
and look at individual
calls, this just

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.430
makes it extremely
evident exactly where

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:47.430
the problem's happening.

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:47.800
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.510
It's all right
here and it's live.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:51.400
So as soon as I
send a new request,

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:54.250
you would see that
request as well.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:58.397
In addition, of course, you have
some of the cool Grafana stuff.

00:11:58.397 --> 00:12:00.730
There's a number of different
dashboards that come with.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:03.190
Obviously, you can
set up your own.

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:07.100
I like to see the
workloads and how

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:10.210
they're responding across time.

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:13.270
And you can see, obviously, I
haven't made a lot of requests

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:14.200
here.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:15.940
But just the requests
that I have made

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:18.935
are showing up
right here for you.

00:12:18.935 --> 00:12:19.810
Oh, sorry about that.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:20.530
CHRIS LATIMER: I mean,
this would be great

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:22.060
if you're somebody
in operations and you

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:23.980
want to be able to look and
see if things are getting out

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:25.555
of the normal, healthy band.

00:12:25.555 --> 00:12:27.047
This, again, just makes it--

00:12:27.047 --> 00:12:28.630
the nice thing about
this is it really

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:30.755
addresses the
individual personas

00:12:30.755 --> 00:12:33.130
and the individual roles that
you have in an organization

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:35.260
and what everybody needs
to see specifically.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.570
SCOTT GANYO: Exactly, exactly.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:42.910
And then we also
have Prometheus.

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:47.530
That also shows you another
view of this information,

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:51.550
and you can see either a
graph or a console of exactly

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:54.160
how the requests came
through and all the log

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:54.827
details on that.

00:12:54.827 --> 00:12:56.326
CHRIS LATIMER: And
again this is all

00:12:56.326 --> 00:12:59.160
being generated in real time,
all through that adapter.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:00.730
You didn't go in
and write code in

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.314
each individual microservice.

00:13:02.314 --> 00:13:03.730
Everything that
you see here, this

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:06.589
is all coming just from the fact
that you plugged your services

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:07.630
into the Istio framework.

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:09.088
SCOTT GANYO: Just
the fact that you

00:13:09.088 --> 00:13:12.170
deployed on Istio, absolutely.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:12.670
OK.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:15.515
So I'm going to hand
it back to you, then.

00:13:15.515 --> 00:13:16.390
CHRIS LATIMER: Great.

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:16.931
Thanks a lot.

00:13:16.931 --> 00:13:19.022
So that's really
service management.

00:13:19.022 --> 00:13:20.980
So you can see there's
a lot of advantages that

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.290
come with Istio out of the box.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:24.970
So we talked a
little bit earlier

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:27.160
about some of the challenges
we have with monoliths

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:28.150
in terms of agility.

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:31.282
You saw Scott show
three different versions

00:13:31.282 --> 00:13:33.240
of that service that was
live at the same time.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.050
Now, he was refreshing
and we were sending

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:36.570
those sort of arbitrarily.

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:38.452
In a normal type of
real world situation,

00:13:38.452 --> 00:13:39.910
what we would want
to do is we want

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:41.160
to look at that service graph.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.050
We would want to understand
where might my next version

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:47.207
cause and breaking changes,
and be more deliberate in terms

00:13:47.207 --> 00:13:48.790
of how we roll those
out and how we're

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:50.122
handling those dependencies.

00:13:50.122 --> 00:13:51.580
But right away,
you can see there's

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:53.980
a lot of benefits that
come from Istio just right

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.315
out of the box.

00:13:55.315 --> 00:13:57.190
But now, what if we want
to take one of those

00:13:57.190 --> 00:13:59.200
services that we
built out in Istio

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.750
and we want to externalize that.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:04.900
We want to turn that into
a more traditional API.

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:07.610
Maybe we want to expose it
within our organization,

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.670
to partners, or we
want to put it out

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:11.742
for anyone on the internet.

00:14:11.742 --> 00:14:13.450
Generally, that's
where we start to think

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.580
about API management
coming into play

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:17.890
and we start to think of
these as more traditional API

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:22.370
management problems to solve.

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.730
And so let's talk a little
bit about API management

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.710
and what it is and what
people are typically

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:29.710
looking at when they want to
implement an API management

00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:31.090
solution.

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:32.740
Usually, we're
thinking about tools

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.740
for humans that are going
to be building applications

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:37.400
using these APIs.

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:40.480
And often that starts with
an API catalog of some sort.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.700
We want to make it
very easy for people

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:46.300
to look into the catalog, browse
through, search through, find

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:48.400
the APIs that are
available, then look

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:51.612
at documentation for those APIs
to understand how it works.

00:14:51.612 --> 00:14:54.070
We usually want to have some
type of self-service mechanism

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:55.870
so that if you decide
this API is going

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:58.060
to work in my
particular application,

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:02.260
I can just simply sign up for
an API key or OAuth credentials,

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:05.390
and I can start to build my
application on top of those.

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:07.420
We also want to
think about features

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:10.540
that are applicable for
an API product manager.

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:13.004
So a lot of times with APIs,
we have different types

00:15:13.004 --> 00:15:14.920
of consumers that are
going to be using these.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:17.440
We may have a
public program where

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:19.990
we want to have a silver,
gold, and platinum

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:20.890
tier of our product.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:23.260
Maybe we want to
monetize those as well.

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:24.837
Or maybe we just
want to productize

00:15:24.837 --> 00:15:26.920
those for different types
of internal and external

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:27.490
consumers.

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:29.590
Maybe we have
applications that we've

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:31.704
built which are maybe our
call center app, maybe

00:15:31.704 --> 00:15:33.370
our mobile app, and
we know that they're

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.602
going to have higher
traffic than some others.

00:15:35.602 --> 00:15:37.060
We want to prioritize
that traffic.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:39.990
We want to give them
higher entitlements to use.

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:42.240
And then, again, from a
product management standpoint,

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:45.400
often we want to look at how
our APIs are actually behaving,

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:46.450
how they're being used.

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:48.790
If we roll out a new feature
in the API, we want to see

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:51.370
are people actually using
this in their applications

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:53.830
and we want to be able to take
advantage of the visibility

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.410
that API management gives us.

00:15:56.410 --> 00:15:58.120
Now, traditionally,
the way that we

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:01.660
tie in our runtime
aspects of APIs

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:03.800
from the configuration
aspects of APIs

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:06.160
is with something
called an API gateway.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.620
And usually these are a
centralized enforcement point

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:10.150
where we might put
these in our DMZ,

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:11.900
we might put these--
the appropriate point

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:15.040
in our network to provide
centralized control over things

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.240
like access control.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:19.390
We might want to have OAuth
or API key validation or job

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:20.680
validation.

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:23.170
We might want to put in
place throttling or threat

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:27.760
protection, edge caching, or
more complex transformations

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:29.050
and mediations.

00:16:29.050 --> 00:16:32.110
And these work great
for traditional APIs.

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:34.090
The question we get
quite a bit is that what

00:16:34.090 --> 00:16:36.250
if I'm in a service
mesh and I already have

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:38.300
a sidecar proxy that's running.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:40.629
It's already
intercepting my traffic.

00:16:40.629 --> 00:16:42.670
I don't necessarily want
to put another component

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:46.480
into my architecture and have
an additional network hop, so

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:49.180
how can we avoid that?

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:52.950
So the good news is we've
built API management for Istio.

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:54.640
And what this
allows you to do is

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:59.200
it allows you to take all of the
runtime capabilities of Istio

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:01.720
and apply policies
from API management.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:04.390
Now, one of the next questions
we often get is, well,

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:06.849
do I need API management
for all my services?

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:07.750
And in general, no.

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:10.173
You want to pick and choose
where it's appropriate.

00:17:10.173 --> 00:17:11.589
So with a lot of
services, you may

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:14.680
have things that are completely
internal to your service mesh.

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:16.501
They may only be called
by other services.

00:17:16.501 --> 00:17:18.250
You never want to take
those capabilities,

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:21.502
open those up, and expose
for people to consume.

00:17:21.502 --> 00:17:22.960
And other times,
you'll have things

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:24.335
that are very
applicable that you

00:17:24.335 --> 00:17:26.076
know you can put
into mobile apps,

00:17:26.076 --> 00:17:28.450
and into other applications
and get all kinds of business

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:29.720
value out of those.

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:31.095
And those are the
places where we

00:17:31.095 --> 00:17:33.830
want to enforce API
management rules.

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:35.350
So how are these different?

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:37.060
Because a lot of times
we'll look at this

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:39.850
and say, well, it seems
like Istio has a gateway,

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.370
Apigee as a gateway.

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:43.935
Istio has some things
for authentication.

00:17:43.935 --> 00:17:44.560
So does Apigee.

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.050
So what are the different
times and approaches

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:50.094
that we might take in one
situation versus another?

00:17:50.094 --> 00:17:51.760
So one of the biggest
things that you'll

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:54.460
see right off the bat is
that with API management,

00:17:54.460 --> 00:17:58.930
we're generally thinking
about HTTP-based APIs, things

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:03.790
that are very specifically at
that L7 layer of the network.

00:18:03.790 --> 00:18:08.300
With Istio, we have more control
over what we can actually

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:08.800
manage.

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:12.020
So we can do things
that the L3, L4 layers,

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:14.360
as well as the L7 layers.

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:16.610
From an authentication
authorization perspective,

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:20.150
Istio is very much interested
in service identities.

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:21.500
So we talked about Citadel.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:23.030
We talked about
the ability to use

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:26.300
MTLS and certificate-based
authentication

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:28.160
and authorization approaches.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:29.870
With API management,
we're generally

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:32.960
thinking about things like OAuth
and we're thinking about jots

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:34.904
and we're thinking
about API keys

00:18:34.904 --> 00:18:36.320
that applications
are going to use

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:39.516
so we can identify those
apps when a call comes in.

00:18:39.516 --> 00:18:41.390
We'll also see differences
in terms of things

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:42.480
like rate limiting.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.210
So from a service
perspective, we're

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:46.100
usually wanting to
put at a service

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:50.030
level rules about how the
service is going to be,

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:51.930
limits around how the
service can be used,

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:53.480
how often it can be called.

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:55.112
And rate limits from
an API perspective

00:18:55.112 --> 00:18:57.320
are often going to be more
around the use case that's

00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:57.820
happening.

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:00.890
So again, we'll see a lot of
companies that are opening up

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:02.130
APIs to the public.

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:03.920
And they want to
have different tiers

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:05.690
and different
monetization packages that

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:06.800
go along with those.

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:09.830
Those are really much more
of it API management problem,

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:12.410
that we want to think about
API management solutions

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:14.520
versus service management.

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:16.270
And then from a portal
perspective, again,

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:19.090
from the main consumer
and service management

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:20.980
is going to be other services.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:23.020
Whereas from an API
management perspective,

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:25.480
we're thinking about people
that are building the apps.

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:26.980
We're really thinking
about the humans that

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:28.780
are out there that
are building apps that

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:30.730
are going to use these APIs.

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:32.470
And for that reason,
we tend to think

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:36.790
of a portal as a more
foundational component of API

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:38.440
management.

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:40.300
So what does this
actually look like when

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:44.159
we think about Apigee
API management and Istio?

00:19:44.159 --> 00:19:46.450
So if we started up with just
a pure service management

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:48.427
approach, we've
got the components

00:19:48.427 --> 00:19:49.510
we've talked about before.

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:54.160
We've got a side car proxy
that we've deployed along

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:55.540
with our microservice.

00:19:55.540 --> 00:19:59.230
We've configured Pilot to send
the necessary configuration

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:01.540
into Mixer and into the proxy.

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:04.420
And we configured Mixer to
be able to enforce those.

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:07.270
Now, if we want to apply
API management rules,

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:10.240
the good news is
Mixer is extensible.

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:12.250
So Mixer gives us
all the mechanisms

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:15.412
that we need to apply
API management rules.

00:20:15.412 --> 00:20:17.620
So if you start looking at
some of those capabilities

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:21.670
we discussed, things like API
key or OAuth validation, jot

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:24.340
validation, performing
some type of remediation

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:29.220
or transformation, quotas,
and analytic pipelines.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:32.529
We can actually avoid having
a separate API gateway

00:20:32.529 --> 00:20:33.945
and we can tie
into the mechanisms

00:20:33.945 --> 00:20:37.110
that Istio gives us using Mixer.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:41.170
And so what we've done is
we've built an adapter.

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:44.340
When I say we, I really mean
you have built an adapter that

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.910
lets us apply all of those
capabilities using the Apigee

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:49.470
platform.

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:52.380
And so again, as interesting
as this picture is,

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:54.855
I think it is much more
interesting if we turn it back

00:20:54.855 --> 00:20:56.730
over to Scott and actually
take a look at how

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:57.470
this works in practice.

00:20:57.470 --> 00:20:58.050
SCOTT GANYO: All right.

00:20:58.050 --> 00:20:58.800
Yep, yep.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:01.008
No, actually, I think that
was a fascinating picture.

00:21:01.008 --> 00:21:02.700
It's actually a
good one if you want

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:07.750
to go back and understand how
all these pieces fit together.

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:10.590
But we're going to go back to
our little Bookinfo application

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:11.230
here.

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:13.500
And the one thing
that was missing

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:16.350
before is we had
an app and people

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:21.600
could go and look up
this book information.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:25.200
But now, if I wanted to write
my own app on top of that,

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:26.130
how would I do that?

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:31.200
Or if I'm the Bookinfo
provider, how do I give that

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:32.679
to other developers?

00:21:32.679 --> 00:21:34.470
And the way we're going
to do that is we're

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:37.140
just going expose,
for our example here,

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:41.000
the details
information as an API.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:48.216
So first off, get over
here and show you.

00:21:48.216 --> 00:21:52.780
What we're going to do first
is expose a virtual service

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:56.830
to allow the details
information to be provided

00:21:56.830 --> 00:22:01.950
to the outside world, outside
of the mesh, the service mesh.

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:06.230
And a virtual service is
really just a set of endpoints

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:08.020
that you want to expose.

00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:11.830
And the default service shows--

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:14.500
you can see here that
you have a product page,

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:15.980
log in, log out,
things like that.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:17.620
All that is part
of the application.

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:20.680
And what we're doing is adding
this little-- oops, sorry--

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:24.660
adding this little bit here
at the bottom which says,

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:31.510
I want to expose
details directly out.

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:36.880
And this will actually go expose
this virtual service here.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:42.880
And all we have to do is
apply that using the Kube,

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.140
the Kube control.

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:46.520
So it's a Kubernetes.

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:48.436
CHRIS LATIMER: So
basically, what you're doing

00:22:48.436 --> 00:22:50.380
is you're telling Istio,
I've got a new API.

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:51.520
It's a virtual service.

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:53.415
Here are the paths
that is going to expect

00:22:53.415 --> 00:22:55.540
that you should be able to
apply policies on these.

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:55.876
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:22:55.876 --> 00:22:58.251
CHRIS LATIMER: Here's the
prefix that you should look at.

00:22:58.251 --> 00:23:01.930
And now you're going to use
kubectl to actually send

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:02.920
that configuration in.

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:03.628
SCOTT GANYO: Yes.

00:23:03.628 --> 00:23:04.360
Yes.

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:06.650
So you can see, I've created--

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:09.590
configured the Bookinfo.

00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:12.580
And now we should be able to
actually do a curl on that.

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:13.970
And this is live, by the way.

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:16.350
This is not made up.

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:20.260
This is an existing
system I installed,

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:22.960
just like it's the same Bookinfo
system that I showed you

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:24.100
in the beginning.

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:27.580
And now we're actually
getting a JSON payload back

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:29.782
from my details page.

00:23:29.782 --> 00:23:31.240
CHRIS LATIMER: And
so now this call

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:33.031
actually went right to
that virtual service

00:23:33.031 --> 00:23:34.470
that you just
configured in Istio.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:34.900
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:35.600
Right.

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:36.850
And service was already there.

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:38.830
I just changed the routing
so I could get to it.

00:23:38.830 --> 00:23:40.670
CHRIS LATIMER: So we showed
that in the previous example.

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:41.670
You had this all set up.

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:42.730
This is the Bookinfo app.

00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:44.860
And in fact, if you
want, we'll show

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:48.160
later you can go into Istio.io
and this is a sample app that

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:49.447
comes with the platform.

00:23:49.447 --> 00:23:50.530
SCOTT GANYO: Right, right.

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:54.480
So so now we have the API.

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:56.580
the Problem, of course,
is nobody knows about it.

00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:58.140
It's unprotected.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:00.140
Nobody knows how to use it.

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:02.500
None of that's good, right?

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:04.690
That's just an accident
waiting to happen.

00:24:04.690 --> 00:24:10.230
So what we want to do is start
managing it through Apigee.

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:12.250
And what I'm going
to do here is--

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:14.920
so this first little bit is
a one-time configuration.

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:19.180
It just tells
Istio about Apigee,

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:20.825
that the adapter exists.

00:24:20.825 --> 00:24:22.450
CHRIS LATIMER: So
this is the mechanism

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:24.990
we talked about the fact
that Mixer is extensible.

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:25.250
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:27.100
CHRIS LATIMER: And so you
built that extension and this

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:28.960
is you telling Istio,
now you want to use

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:30.389
that extension in your mesh.

00:24:30.389 --> 00:24:31.180
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.640
So the first part
here just declares

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:37.120
that there is an Apigee
adapter in the configuration.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:40.390
The next one defines a
template for my analytics

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:43.600
because I want to pull
information out of Istio

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:45.760
and give it to Apigee
so that it also

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:49.490
can report on this information.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:50.930
There's an instance
configuration

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:54.870
for that that just tells
us which attributes

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.850
of out of Istio
we want, and then

00:24:57.850 --> 00:24:59.980
a template for
authorization that tells us

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:04.572
which attributes we're looking
for, for example, an API key.

00:25:04.572 --> 00:25:06.030
CHRIS LATIMER: So
that right there,

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:08.689
that's the hook in that we
have from sort of the Istio

00:25:08.689 --> 00:25:10.980
configuration into what Apigee
is expecting so we can--

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:11.940
SCOTT GANYO: Right, right.

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:12.470
CHRIS LATIMER: --apply
those policies.

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:15.240
SCOTT GANYO: It's part of what
you might call installation

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:16.260
is to set this up.

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:22.510
The next thing we want to do
is we define an Apigee handler.

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:24.300
So now, this tells us--

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:26.770
this tells Istio the
adapter that we just

00:25:26.770 --> 00:25:32.240
created, how to connect
to an Apigee environment.

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:34.630
So you have an organization
and environment set up

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:42.010
as part of Apigee that
actually tells your system

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:46.720
how to do the authentication
and authorization, quota,

00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:47.980
and things like that.

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:53.530
And this will actually
connect that, create it.

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:56.680
And then, finally, we're
going to define a rule.

00:25:56.680 --> 00:26:01.690
And what the rule does is
tells Istio, all right,

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:05.780
under these conditions
apply Apigee management.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:07.990
And in this case, we're
just-- it's a simple rule.

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:11.340
We're going to say, when the
destination service is details.

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:13.770
So it won't affect the
rest, but when it's details,

00:26:13.770 --> 00:26:17.460
we're going to send
authorization the Apigee

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:19.440
and send the
analytics to Apigee.

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:21.134
CHRIS LATIMER: So
you took the handler,

00:26:21.134 --> 00:26:22.800
so that's basically
the request handler.

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:24.930
And you're telling
Istio that this is how

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:25.710
we're going to handle requests.

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:27.160
And then you configure
this rule that says, here

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:29.040
are the rules that we
actually want to apply--

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:29.340
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:26:29.340 --> 00:26:29.430
When

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:31.263
CHRIS LATIMER: --when
see a request matching

00:26:31.263 --> 00:26:32.610
these specific conditions.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:33.540
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:38.730
So now at this point, Apigee
is in control of that API.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:41.360
So if we attempt to
access that, we're

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:44.140
going to hit the authentication
authorization, quota

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:48.470
management, and it's
going to report on that.

00:26:48.470 --> 00:26:51.590
And let's actually,
first off, let's look

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:54.680
at what Apigee
actually looks like

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:58.850
and how we define
that information here.

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:01.370
As you can see, I've
set up this already.

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:04.340
So this is a product that
I've set up for developers

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:05.760
to be able to access.

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:09.570
And you can see here
that when you set it up,

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:11.070
you can set up
multiple environments

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.100
and apply them as you wish.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:16.387
Now, Mixer only attaches
to one of the environments.

00:27:16.387 --> 00:27:18.970
So you can have two versions of
it or whatever you want to do,

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:21.860
but you have that access.

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:24.020
You have key
approval, which we'll

00:27:24.020 --> 00:27:26.240
see when you get to the portal.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:27.732
You have a quota.

00:27:27.732 --> 00:27:29.690
I set up a quota of five
requests every minute.

00:27:29.690 --> 00:27:32.519
That's pretty low, but we
want to see how that works.

00:27:32.519 --> 00:27:34.310
CHRIS LATIMER: It's
easier than showing you

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:35.660
1,000 requests per second.

00:27:35.660 --> 00:27:36.240
SCOTT GANYO: Exactly.

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:36.715
Right, exactly

00:27:36.715 --> 00:27:38.140
CHRIS LATIMER: Scott trying
to click 1,000 times.

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:39.550
So we talked about
productization.

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:41.000
So this is the
mechanism in Apigee.

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:42.060
We have the productization
and API products.

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:42.200
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:43.730
And this also is live.

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:48.500
So if I change it, it will be
automatically updated in Istio.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.300
And you also have
resource paths.

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:52.305
And down here you can see--

00:27:52.305 --> 00:27:54.320
it's a little bit
small, perhaps.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:58.610
But you can see that the service
that I want to connect to

00:27:58.610 --> 00:27:59.730
is listed there.

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:03.290
So I say that I'm going to the
details.default dot service

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:04.360
cluster local.

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:06.830
That's just a
Kubernetes DNS name.

00:28:06.830 --> 00:28:07.280
CHRIS LATIMER: That's
the service and where

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:08.200
you can find the service.

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:09.020
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:11.670
And it tells us right there.

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:19.140
So let's go back here and
now that we've protected it,

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:21.210
we can't get to it.

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:24.570
So nobody can get to it
without an API key anymore.

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:26.280
Well, so how do we
get the API key?

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:29.760
Well, I mentioned the
developer portal--

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:32.152
sorry.

00:28:32.152 --> 00:28:33.360
So let's take a look at that.

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:36.290
Now, I've already
created a portal.

00:28:36.290 --> 00:28:39.660
And basically, it just involves
selecting a few things,

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:42.450
selecting which products
you want to make available.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:44.710
And I've logged in.

00:28:44.710 --> 00:28:46.810
So I you to see the app.

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:48.540
So I created an app
and said, OK, I want

00:28:48.540 --> 00:28:50.700
to access the details page.

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:57.690
And my app then says,
OK, I have access here

00:28:57.690 --> 00:29:03.780
to this product, the Bookinfo
details and I have a key here.

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:05.340
And I have a secret.

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:09.000
So normally, I would take
this key and copy it.

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:12.420
I've already done
that for us here.

00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:16.555
And you can see here's
the key that we got.

00:29:16.555 --> 00:29:18.930
And I'm going to run the same
command, only this time I'm

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:22.354
going to pass it an API key.

00:29:22.354 --> 00:29:23.520
And now I have access again.

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:24.240
CHRIS LATIMER: Ta-da.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:25.031
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:29:25.031 --> 00:29:27.200
So now we've protected
it, which is great.

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:33.076
And authorization is done there,
too, as far as path access

00:29:33.076 --> 00:29:33.950
and things like that.

00:29:33.950 --> 00:29:36.380
I allowed any path.

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:39.710
So now developers
can hit our APIs.

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:41.041
That's cool.

00:29:41.041 --> 00:29:42.290
So what do we know about them?

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.180
Well, now we can
go back into Apigee

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:45.920
and we can look at
developer engagement

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:49.967
and we can see that, actually,
this is my developer here.

00:29:49.967 --> 00:29:51.050
It's a little small again.

00:29:51.050 --> 00:29:53.000
Sorry.

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:58.670
And we can see other things
like proxy performance.

00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:03.420
And we can narrow that down
to just the details page

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:04.650
and see that information.

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:06.240
CHRIS LATIMER: And this isn't
an either/or proposition.

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:08.040
We can still get Istio
analytics on this service.

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:08.300
SCOTT GANYO: Absolutely.

00:30:08.300 --> 00:30:09.360
All of that is still happening.

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:09.840
CHRIS LATIMER: We
can also now augment

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:11.261
that with Apigee's analytics.

00:30:11.261 --> 00:30:12.510
SCOTT GANYO: Absolutely, 100%.

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:13.551
CHRIS LATIMER: Very cool.

00:30:13.551 --> 00:30:14.900
SCOTT GANYO: Yeah.

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:15.600
All right.

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:19.200
So let's get back.

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:20.616
We have the quota.

00:30:20.616 --> 00:30:21.990
So here I set up
a little script.

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:24.830
It's going to hit it a
bunch of times really quick.

00:30:24.830 --> 00:30:25.385
And--

00:30:25.385 --> 00:30:26.510
CHRIS LATIMER: There we go.

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:27.468
SCOTT GANYO: All right.

00:30:27.468 --> 00:30:31.210
So for this key, my
developer can only

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:33.040
access it five times a minute.

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:36.960
And each one, each developer
has that same quota.

00:30:36.960 --> 00:30:38.570
So what have we done?

00:30:38.570 --> 00:30:42.090
We've exposed the API,
published it to the portal,

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:44.860
required a key to access it.

00:30:44.860 --> 00:30:47.430
We allowed developers to
register and get those keys.

00:30:47.430 --> 00:30:50.700
And we can set per
developer quota limits.

00:30:50.700 --> 00:30:51.960
So one more thing--

00:30:51.960 --> 00:30:54.210
I think we have time.

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:57.100
I just want to show you
that we can also OAuth.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:00.930
So here's an OAuth
definition, and I'm not going

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:02.350
to spend too much time on it.

00:31:02.350 --> 00:31:07.040
You can specify an issuer and
cert URL and things like that.

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.340
I'm going to apply that.

00:31:09.340 --> 00:31:11.220
Now, I need to generate a token.

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:14.790
Part of that is I need
to not only have the key,

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:16.860
but now I need to
have the secret.

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:23.180
So here, put the secret in here.

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:25.670
And this is just a little
script that generates a token

00:31:25.670 --> 00:31:26.810
and does the call.

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:29.120
And you can see that
I'm just doing the curl

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:31.250
again, this time
with a bare token.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:36.760
So you see, a
token was generated

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:39.360
and something didn't work.

00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:41.820
Let me just try it again.

00:31:41.820 --> 00:31:43.490
All right.

00:31:43.490 --> 00:31:45.112
It doesn't like something.

00:31:45.112 --> 00:31:46.100
CHRIS LATIMER: That's
still pretty good.

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:46.680
SCOTT GANYO: I don't
think we have--

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:47.030
CHRIS LATIMER: That's
still pretty good.

00:31:47.030 --> 00:31:48.000
We've got all the
way through the demo.

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:49.430
There's been only one hiccup.

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:51.330
SCOTT GANYO: We don't have
time to debug that right now.

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:52.940
CHRIS LATIMER: I'm going
to count that as a success,

00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:53.690
regardless.

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:54.648
SCOTT GANYO: All right.

00:31:54.648 --> 00:31:57.095
So let's get back
to wrapping up here.

00:31:57.095 --> 00:31:58.970
CHRIS LATIMER: So
hopefully, after this demo,

00:31:58.970 --> 00:32:01.760
you can see you've got a
lot of great capabilities

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:02.780
that come with Istio.

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:05.090
It's going to solve a lot
of those core microservices

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:07.190
problems that you're
going to run into as you

00:32:07.190 --> 00:32:08.714
try to scale microservices.

00:32:08.714 --> 00:32:10.130
And then inevitably,
when you need

00:32:10.130 --> 00:32:11.588
to take one of
those micro services

00:32:11.588 --> 00:32:13.640
you need to expose
that out as an API

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:15.440
and you need traditional
API management,

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:19.262
we've made it very easy with
API management for Istio

00:32:19.262 --> 00:32:21.856
to tie into all the
capabilities of Apigee

00:32:21.856 --> 00:32:23.480
and build that right
into the platform.

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:25.660
So you really have a
seamless developer experience

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:28.240
that you can take advantage of.

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:31.240
Now, I'm sure many of you are
