00:34:57.187 --> 00:34:58.770
So Scott, I'll direct
this one at you.

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:02.200
So does Istio work for
workloads running on VMs?

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:03.860
If yes, can you
please explain how?

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:04.610
SCOTT GANYO: Yeah.

00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:06.470
Well, absolutely, it does.

00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:13.490
It's designed to be independent
of Kubernetes, mostly.

00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:17.720
What you would end up doing
is, through the mesh expansion

00:35:17.720 --> 00:35:22.040
abilities, you can
actually run the proxy

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:26.150
next to any container, anywhere.

00:35:26.150 --> 00:35:30.440
It just has to have access
back to the main Istio--

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:33.010
the Pilot has to be
able to configure it.

00:35:33.010 --> 00:35:36.500
And it has to be able
to access the Mixer

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:39.002
and the other components
of the system.

00:35:39.002 --> 00:35:40.460
CHRIS LATIMER: So
yeah, absolutely.

00:35:40.460 --> 00:35:42.460
If you're running most
of your workloads on VMs,

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:45.260
you're not on Kubernetes
yet, Istio is still

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:47.890
extremely valuable for you.

00:35:47.890 --> 00:35:49.494
So let's jump to the second one.

00:35:49.494 --> 00:35:51.160
What is the difference
between analytics

00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:55.136
provided by Apigee and
the one provided by Istio?

00:35:55.136 --> 00:35:57.010
So again, I think we
actually saw quite a bit

00:35:57.010 --> 00:35:58.280
of this in the demo.

00:35:58.280 --> 00:36:01.720
So some of the things that we
saw on there were Istio has--

00:36:01.720 --> 00:36:03.730
so the way Istio
analytics work are really

00:36:03.730 --> 00:36:05.355
with that telemetry adapter.

00:36:05.355 --> 00:36:07.480
And you can take that data
that's coming into Istio

00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:09.210
and you can put it
wherever you'd like.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:11.215
So we've used that same
mechanism in Apigee

00:36:11.215 --> 00:36:12.590
to take the data
that's coming in

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:14.710
and feed into Apigee
analytic system.

00:36:14.710 --> 00:36:18.370
But we also saw Jaeger,
we saw Prometheus.

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:21.100
And depending on your
role in the organization,

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:24.040
Istio gives you a way to
get access and visibility

00:36:24.040 --> 00:36:25.610
into the information you need.

00:36:25.610 --> 00:36:27.130
So if you're an
API product manager

00:36:27.130 --> 00:36:28.930
and you want to look
at the analytics that

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:31.240
are coming into Apigee,
you've got all the dashboards

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:32.350
that are in Apigee.

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:34.930
If you are a mesh
administrator and you're

00:36:34.930 --> 00:36:38.320
looking for ways to track
the dependencies across all

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:41.320
of your mesh, we've got that
in there as well with Istio.

00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:45.610
And then, of course, we saw
from the Grafana dashboards

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:47.980
that you looked at a way
for operations people

00:36:47.980 --> 00:36:51.550
to get a very quick way to
determine what traffic's

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:53.290
coming in, if
things are suddenly

00:36:53.290 --> 00:36:55.060
getting out of the normal
range that we expect to see.

00:36:55.060 --> 00:36:55.120
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:36:55.120 --> 00:36:56.810
And I think one
of the key details

00:36:56.810 --> 00:36:59.650
is Apigee provides
a higher level view.

00:36:59.650 --> 00:37:03.340
It's more tied to your
developers and the analytics

00:37:03.340 --> 00:37:09.526
around them, how the product
is being used and utilized.

00:37:09.526 --> 00:37:11.400
CHRIS LATIMER: So let's
jump to the next one.

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:13.233
I'll let you handle
this one as well, Scott.

00:37:13.233 --> 00:37:16.884
So what kind of data related
to Apigee is cached at envoy?

00:37:16.884 --> 00:37:19.050
And maybe we could expand
this to be not just envoy,

00:37:19.050 --> 00:37:20.645
but maybe anywhere in Istio.

00:37:20.645 --> 00:37:21.770
SCOTT GANYO: Yeah, exactly.

00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:23.560
So we don't really
cache data at envoy.

00:37:23.560 --> 00:37:24.060
No.

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:33.180
When you-- the only exception
is if you install the OAuth bit.

00:37:33.180 --> 00:37:36.420
The envoy actually does
get the public certificate

00:37:36.420 --> 00:37:40.800
for validating your jot
tokens, but that's not

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:42.300
even Apigee-specific.

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:45.930
That's just how it
works, how Istio works.

00:37:45.930 --> 00:37:49.070
We do cache some data in Mixer.

00:37:49.070 --> 00:37:53.570
We cache information
about the API keys,

00:37:53.570 --> 00:37:59.970
the jot tokens for the lifetime,
and some product information.

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:04.620
And this is so that we can
respond immediately and not

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:07.740
get in the way of requests
traveling through Istio.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:10.080
We don't want to go up
to the Apigee server

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:11.860
for every request, as
you can understand.

00:38:11.860 --> 00:38:14.720
That would add a lot
of wanted latency.

00:38:14.720 --> 00:38:16.560
CHRIS LATIMER: Definitely.

00:38:16.560 --> 00:38:18.370
So let's talk about
the next one here.

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:22.230
What are the best ways to secure
communication between Apigee

00:38:22.230 --> 00:38:23.730
and the Istio ingress?

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:25.920
And I'm going to say this
is pretty similar to just

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:28.320
like anything else
within Apigee where

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:30.450
we need to secure the
communication between Apigee

00:38:30.450 --> 00:38:31.620
and the back end.

00:38:31.620 --> 00:38:33.360
We're going to use
standard techniques.

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:35.290
We can use things
like IP whitelisting.

00:38:35.290 --> 00:38:38.340
We can use mutual
TLS, one-way TLS.

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:40.380
All the things that you've
come to know and love

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:42.180
in terms of network security
that have been around

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:43.680
for a long time are
still applicable

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:45.169
here right in today's world.

00:38:45.169 --> 00:38:45.960
SCOTT GANYO: Right.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:47.910
And out of the box.

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:53.590
It's, of course, on using
HTTPS for communication.

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:57.690
It actually does communicate
with authentication-- its own

00:38:57.690 --> 00:38:58.650
authentication.

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:01.050
I showed you in the
handler the authentication

00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:04.900
that you give to
Istio, to the adapter

00:39:04.900 --> 00:39:11.354
so that it can authenticate with
the Apigee on there as well.

00:39:11.354 --> 00:39:13.395
CHRIS LATIMER: So the last
